<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key>
  <string>com.huffmanwrites.ollama-app</string>
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/usr/bin/open</string>
    <string>-a</string>
    <string>/Applications/Ollama.app</string>
  </array>
  <!-- Opens the Ollama desktop app at login so its server is up before the
       scheduled AI jobs run. Seven jobs (the five report runners, wiki-check,
       repair-plan) call http://localhost:11434, and that port is served by the
       app, not by a daemon of our own.

       Why this exists rather than the app's own login item. The app can register
       itself as a login item (SMAppService), and on 2026-10-07 it was added that
       way and verified enabled. It did not survive: a plain restart of the app
       dropped the enrolment, and the app's own strings show why ("removing
       dangling login item", "removing login item") — it reconciles its login
       items on start and removed one it considered stale. A GUI setting that a
       restart can silently clear is not a durable answer for an unattended
       publish, so the enrolment is done here instead, in a file that lives in
       git and is checked by scripts/check-plists.py.

       RunAtLoad and NOT KeepAlive, deliberately. This job starts the app; it does
       not keep it alive. KeepAlive would relaunch the app every time it was quit,
       which fights the person using it. If the app is closed on purpose or dies,
       the run fails loudly instead: scripts/ollama-probe.sh runs in every AI
       runner before its writer and raises the failure alert if the server is
       unreachable.

       It launches the app, never a second server. That distinction is the whole
       reason this file exists: an earlier LaunchAgent ran `ollama serve` directly
       and duplicated the server the app already provides on the same port. `open`
       cannot do that; it hands the bundle to LaunchServices, which starts the app
       if it is not already running and otherwise just brings it forward. One
       `ollama serve` process either way. -->
  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key>
  <string>/Users/prh/Library/Logs/ollama-app.out.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
  <string>/Users/prh/Library/Logs/ollama-app.err.log</string>
</dict>
</plist>
